From c80822a94d896b6136900010d184b94898836c22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 08:24:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09278/11362] Wire an opt-in thinking budget for induction,
 default off

On a reasoning generator the think channel is nearly all of the tokens. Measured
induction completions this session ran 38,471 to 94,459 while the final answer was
1-8k, and induction is the dominant cost of the scored eval.

llama.cpp accepts a per-request `thinking_budget_tokens` (read into reasoning_budget
in tools/server/server-common.cpp) that forces `</think>` after N think tokens and
lets the model still answer. This is NOT capping `max_tokens`: that hard-truncates
mid-answer and produced dead cells in earlier work (overran=True). One closes the
tag, the other cuts the output. A test pins that they stay separate fields.

DEFAULT OFF, pinned by test. Unset leaves the request byte-identical to before this
commit; a malformed or non-positive value falls back to off rather than sending a
field the server may reject, because a broken env var must not fail every induction.

Wired but deliberately NOT enabled or A/B'd. Independent review of the priorities
argues against using it soon: it mitigates a long-completion generator, and on the
incumbent gemma-4-31B thinking is a small share of wall time while exp6221 measures
/think as BETTER on held-out accuracy (0.196 vs 0.083). Capping it there fights that
evidence. So this lands as a measurable knob, not a behaviour change; whether to use
it follows the generator decision.

FRESHNESS ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS, and what they cost. Editing this module invalidated 21
hash-pinned acknowledgements at once, because an acknowledgement stops applying the
moment its dependency changes -- which is the mechanism working, not a fault. Seven
artifacts went stale and each now carries a new acknowledgement naming the exact
hash, the reason, and the evidence.

Worth recording the friction: clearing the backlog by pinning hashes means the next
editor of a widely-depended-on module pays seven acknowledgements for a five-line
default-off change. That is a real maintenance cost of the approach, and it will
recur.

Also worth recording that my first attempt at those acknowledgements was REJECTED.
The guard requires four fields -- path, sha256_now, reason and evidence -- and I
supplied three. It refused silently-but-correctly rather than accepting what its own
source calls "a silent hash bump wearing a costume". The evidence field now names how
inertness was established: the default-off test, the absence of any modified symbol
or branch, and the fact that neither the artifacts nor their build commands set the
variable.

Committed with hooks running, no bypass. 7 tests.
